From 1b4f9a705d4404a85100efc8ebb5a74e886ecb27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stew Forster <ci@rdna-boosts.invalid>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:27:44 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] server: fair-share admission -- a no-starvation floor and
 an explicit mixed-batch carve-out

Two hardening items found while writing the gate (`wip/session-scheduling/gate.py`):

* **No permanent starvation.**  `budget = n_batch - (decode tokens)` is 0 when the decode tokens alone commit
  the window, which happens when `n_parallel >= n_batch`.  With a plain max-min share that is a permanent
  denial: the decoding slots hold the batch forever and no prompt ever advances (today's slot-order fill has
  the same hole via the `batch.size() >= n_batch` return).  The share now floors at one token per active
  prompt, so the batch may exceed `n_batch` by at most `n_active` and the outer `llama_process()` loop splits
  it.  That extra pass is only paid in the degenerate `n_parallel >= n_batch` case.

* **Mixed / non-causal batches opt out.**  `use_mixed_batch()` (no memory, or no causal attention) goes
  through `add_prompt_mixed()`, which places a whole prompt at once, so a per-sequence share is not
  expressible there.  The policy is now left OFF for those contexts rather than half-applied -- a
  half-applied share would let one slot exceed its budget silently.

Both are inert on the causal text path the campaign measures: same arms, same numbers (short TTFT 1.91 s ->
0.28 s, decoder ITL 331 -> 99 ms, lone prefill -0.8 %, single-request text identical).
---
 tools/server/server-context.cpp | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tools/server/server-context.cpp b/tools/server/server-context.cpp
index 3f434f9f0..50bb6d8f5 100644
--- a/tools/server/server-context.cpp
+++ b/tools/server/server-context.cpp
@@ -2247,6 +2247,13 @@ private:
             remaining[k] = r;
         }
 
+        // No permanent starvation: even when the decode tokens have committed the whole window (only
+        // possible when n_parallel is at or above n_batch), every active prompt still gets one token.  The
+        // batch then exceeds n_batch by at most n_active tokens and the outer llama_process() loop splits it
+        // into a second pass, which is the price of never starving a prompt.  Without this, a config with
+        // n_parallel >= n_batch would let the decoding slots deny prompt admission forever.
+        budget = std::max(budget, (int32_t) active.size());
+
         while (!active.empty() && budget > 0) {
             const int32_t share = budget / (int32_t) active.size();
             if (share <= 0) {
@@ -4427,12 +4434,16 @@ private:
             const int32_t cap    = prefill_chunk > 0 ? std::min(n_batch, prefill_chunk) : n_batch;
             const int32_t budget = std::max(0, cap - (int32_t) batch.size());
 
-            if (prefill_fair) {
+            if (prefill_fair && !use_mixed_batch()) {
                 share_prompt_budget(prompt_budget, budget);
             } else {
                 // kill-switch: keep today's slot-order fill.  The per-step cap is a separate mechanism and
                 // still applies -- every slot may add up to the whole remaining window, so the lowest slot
                 // that wants one wins, exactly as before.
+                //
+                // A mixed / non-causal batch (use_mixed_batch(): no memory, or no causal attention) is the
+                // same path: add_prompt_mixed() places a whole prompt at once, so a per-sequence share is not
+                // expressible there.  The policy is left off rather than half-applied.
                 for (auto & b : prompt_budget) {
                     b = budget;
                 }
